99:Person-Based Similarity Index . The personalised estimates of interindividual variability were investigated using the PBSI, calculated according to the formula below, for each SA, CT and SV pro /uniFB01 les [34 -37]. The process for calculating the PBSI scores begins with concatenating the respective regional measures into vectors that represent the pro /uniFB01 le of each speci /uniFB01 c brain phenotype; PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV, respectively. This produces a simpli /uniFB01 ed, personalised index for each phenotypic neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le. This index can then be used in one of two ways; /uniFB01 rst, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is to that of other individuals with the same clinical pro /uniFB01 le or disorder (within-diagnosis or within-group). Second, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is respective to a normative estimate, i.e. the average of the healthy control group (normativeness) [37].
106:- (ii) Normative reference: Next, the respective neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les of each CHR-P individual were correlated with the corresponding pro /uniFB01 les of the members of the HC group, for each brain phenotype. The resulting PBSI scores thus represent the degree of deviation from the ' normative ' range and were transformed into z -scores (PBSI-CT-Z, PBSI-SA-Z, and PBSI-SV-Z). We set &gt;1.5 SD as a threshold to identify individuals at CHR-P who most markedly deviated from the normative neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le, in line with previous work [37].
136:Normative PBSI . Of the 1579 CHR-P participants, 11 (0.70%) demonstrated marked deviation in PBSI-SA-Z scores, 118 (7.47%) in PBSI-CT-Z and 161 (10.20%) in PBSI-SV-Z (Fig. 5). Of these participants, 17 demonstrated marked deviation in more than one phenotypic pro /uniFB01 le and just one participant in all three phenotypic pro /uniFB01 les. There were no signi /uniFB01 cant associations between normative PBSI scores and transition or subgroup status, or baseline psychopathology (all p &gt;0.18). A signi /uniFB01 cant association with typical antipsychotic use was identi /uniFB01 ed for the PBSI-SV-Z scores ( b = -0.84, t (1220) = -2.191, p = 0.03), with antipsychotic use being associated with greater deviations from PSBI-SV-Z. No association with antipsychotic use was identi /uniFB01 ed with PBSI-SA-Z or PBIS-CT-Z scores.
